{% from "partials/icons.njk" import icon %}
{#
  The script syntax, shown rather than described: the same passage as you type
  it and as CueCard renders it. Two panels, side by side on a wide screen and
  stacked on a narrow one, with the second one live - script.js counts the
  timestamp down, so the difference between the columns is visible rather than
  claimed.

  The two apps do not read the same tags. The desktop app understands
  [time mm:ss] as well as cues; the phone app's parser handles cues only and
  takes its timer from the app rather than from the script. Set
  syntaxKind = "mobile" to show the phone's version, which is the honest one on
  every /mobile/ page.

  The classes .timestamp and .action-tag are load-bearing: script.js finds them.
#}
{% set mobileSyntax = syntaxKind == "mobile" %}
<section class="features syntax-notes{% if syntaxSolid %} solid{% endif %}" id="syntax" aria-labelledby="syntax-h">
    <div class="wrap">
        <p class="micro section-tag">{{ icon("pen", 13) }}{{ syntaxMicro or "The script" }}</p>
        <h2 class="section-h section-title" id="syntax-h">{{ syntaxHeading or "Write it plainly. Read it like a pro." }}</h2>
        <p class="body">
            {% if syntaxBody %}{{ syntaxBody | safe }}
            {% elif mobileSyntax %}Type your script the way you would say it. One optional tag does the rest: a cue, which reminds you how to say the next line and is drawn in its own colour so you take it in without reading it out.
            {% else %}Type your script the way you would say it. Two optional tags do the rest: a timestamp to keep you honest about length, and a cue to remind you how to say the next line — shown to you, never spoken.{% endif %}
        </p>

        <div class="features-grid">
            <div class="feature-card">
                <div class="feature-header">
                    <span class="feature-number row-n">01</span>
                    <h3 class="feature-title">What you write</h3>
                </div>
                <p class="feature-desc">
                    Plain text, plus <code>[cue]</code>{% if not mobileSyntax %} and <code>[time]</code>{% endif %} wherever they help.
                    The older <code>[note]</code> spelling still works.
                </p>
                <div class="notes-preview notes-preview-raw" aria-label="Sample of a raw CueCard script">
                    <div class="notes-content">{% if not mobileSyntax %}[time 00:55]

                        {% endif %}Imagine your notes floating above the deck. [cue smile] I've
                        been using CueCard to do that.

                        It has a [cue emphasize] timer and that sub-note thingy,
                        which [cue shrug a little] makes me sound way more human.

                        [cue widen the eyes] You gotta try this out.
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="feature-card">
                <div class="feature-header">
                    <span class="feature-number row-n">02</span>
                    <h3 class="feature-title">What you see</h3>
                </div>
                <p class="feature-desc">
                    {% if mobileSyntax %}Cues are lit in the colour you picked and the word you are on is highlighted as the script scrolls, so your eye catches them without your mouth doing.
                    {% else %}Timestamps count down on screen and cues are lit in their own colour, so your eye catches them without your mouth doing.{% endif %}
                </p>
                <div class="notes-preview notes-preview-rendered" aria-live="off">
                    <div class="notes-content">{% if not mobileSyntax %}<span class="timestamp" data-time="55">[00:55]</span>

                        {% endif %}Imagine your notes floating above the deck. <span class="action-tag">[smile]</span> I've
                        been using CueCard to do that.

                        It has a <span class="action-tag">[emphasize]</span> timer and that sub-note thingy,
                        which <span class="action-tag">[shrug a little]</span> makes me sound way more human.

                        <span class="action-tag">[widen the eyes]</span> You gotta try this out.
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>
